//? Title: Segment intersection: exact contact decisions and approximate constructed points
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Decide whether two closed line segments meet and distinguish a unique point from
a nonzero collinear overlap. Actual exact orientation signs precede the original
crossing, disjoint and shared-endpoint tests against the diagonal from(0,0)
to(6,6).
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

The orientation sign of (q-p) cross (r-p) identifies which side of a directed
line contains r. Four exact nonzero opposite signs give a proper straddle. Zero
signs require closed coordinate bounding checks. Collinear intersection is the
common lexicographically ordered endpoint interval: empty, a single point or a
nonzero overlap. The canonical dyadic integer kernel computes exact signs for
finite stored binary64 coordinates, with no epsilon. A proper intersection point
additionally uses a widened floating quotient of exactly accumulated
determinants. Exact classification does not make that final interior coordinate
an exact or correctly rounded rational representation.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/segment_intersection_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    namespace z = dm::geometry;
    auto r = z::segment_intersection_trace({0, 0}, {2, 2}, {0, 2}, {2, 0});
    std::print("Actual exact signs `{}`, actual orientation calls {}, contact enum {}, constructed point " "({},{}), parameter {}.\n",
               r.orientations, r.orientation_tests, int(r.contact), r.first.x,
               r.first.y, r.parameter);
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "Two diagonals in a 2 by 2 square");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("Actual exact signs {}", r.orientations));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0},
                         std::format("Actual unique point ({},{})", r.first.x,
                                     r.first.y));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/segment_intersection_tiny.svg");
}
```
