> API and interpretation

The boolean predicate avoids approximate point construction entirely. A unique
collinear endpoint or point-segment contact now returns its actual endpoint; a
nonzero overlap retains two bounds and cannot be represented by one point.
Infinite-line intersections remain distinct from segment intersections.
False/error leaves legacy output arguments unchanged. Exactness refers to the
supplied stored coordinates, not unrounded physical measurements.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Shewchuk, Adaptive Precision Floating-Point Arithmetic and Fast Robust Geometric Predicates (1997), robustness motivation; fixed dyadic integer variant here|https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~quake/robust.html]

